    "summary": "Swiss computer scientist (1934–2024) Niklaus Wirth Wirth in 2005 Born Niklaus Emil Wirth ( 1934-02-15 ) 15 February 1934 Winterthur , Switzerland Died 1 January 2024 (2024-01-01) (aged 89) Zürich , Switzerland Education ETH Zurich ( BS ) Laval University ( MS ) UC Berkeley ( PhD ) Known for Oberon Oberon-2 Oberon-07 Oberon System Euler Pascal ALGOL W Modula Modula-2 Children 3 Awards IEEE Emanuel R. Piore Award (1983) Turing Award (1984) SIGPLAN Programming Languages Achievement Award Fellow of the Computer History Museum (2004) Marcel Benoist Prize (1989) Scientific career Fields Computer science Workplaces ETH Zurich Stanford University University of Zurich Xerox PARC Thesis A Generalization of Algol (1963) Doctoral advisor Harry Huskey Edward Feigenbaum Doctoral students Martin Odersky Michael Franz Signature Niklaus Emil Wirth ( IPA : / v ɛr t / ) (15 February 1934 – 1 January 2024) was a Swiss computer scientist . He designed several programming languages , including Pascal , and pioneered several classic topics in software engineering. In 1984, he won the Turing Award , generally recognized as the highest distinction in computer science , \"for developing a sequence of innovative computer languages\". ^ Gosch, John (25 October 1979). Weber, Samuel (ed.). \"Wirth works to better Pascal\" (PDF) . Profile. Electronics . Paul W. Reiss. p. 157. ISSN 0013-5070 . Archived (PDF) from the original on 20 May 2024 . Retrieved 14 August 2024 . [his family] includes two girls and a boy ^ \"IEEE Emanuel R. Piore Award Recipients\" (PDF) . IEEE . Archived from the original (PDF) on 24 November 2010 . Retrieved 20 March 2021 . ^ \"Niklaus Wirth 2004 Fellow\" . Computer History Museum . Archived from the original on 3 July 2019 . Retrieved 1 December 2017 . ^ \"Niklaus E. Wirth - A.M. Turing Award Laureate\" . Association for Computing Machinery. 2019. Archived from the original on 29 June 2017 . Retrieved 8 January 2024 .",
    "text": "Swiss computer scientist (1934–2024) Niklaus Wirth Wirth in 2005 Born Niklaus Emil Wirth ( 1934-02-15 ) 15 February 1934 Winterthur , Switzerland Died 1 January 2024 (2024-01-01) (aged 89) Zürich , Switzerland Education ETH Zurich ( BS ) Laval University ( MS ) UC Berkeley ( PhD ) Known for Oberon Oberon-2 Oberon-07 Oberon System Euler Pascal ALGOL W Modula Modula-2 Children 3 Awards IEEE Emanuel R. Piore Award (1983) Turing Award (1984) SIGPLAN Programming Languages Achievement Award Fellow of the Computer History Museum (2004) Marcel Benoist Prize (1989) Scientific career Fields Computer science Workplaces ETH Zurich Stanford University University of Zurich Xerox PARC Thesis A Generalization of Algol (1963) Doctoral advisor Harry Huskey Edward Feigenbaum Doctoral students Martin Odersky Michael Franz Signature Niklaus Emil Wirth ( IPA : / v ɛr t / ) (15 February 1934 – 1 January 2024) was a Swiss computer scientist . He designed several programming languages , including Pascal , and pioneered several classic topics in software engineering. In 1984, he won the Turing Award , generally recognized as the highest distinction in computer science , \"for developing a sequence of innovative computer languages\". ^ Gosch, John (25 October 1979). Weber, Samuel (ed.). \"Wirth works to better Pascal\" (PDF) . Profile. Electronics . Paul W. Reiss. p. 157. ISSN 0013-5070 . Archived (PDF) from the original on 20 May 2024 . Retrieved 14 August 2024 . [his family] includes two girls and a boy ^ \"IEEE Emanuel R. Piore Award Recipients\" (PDF) . IEEE . Archived from the original (PDF) on 24 November 2010 . Retrieved 20 March 2021 . ^ \"Niklaus Wirth 2004 Fellow\" . Computer History Museum . Archived from the original on 3 July 2019 . Retrieved 1 December 2017 . ^ \"Niklaus E. Wirth - A.M. Turing Award Laureate\" . Association for Computing Machinery. 2019. Archived from the original on 29 June 2017 . Retrieved 8 January 2024 .",
